The main pattern is that forecast growth produces very different margin outcomes by site: Hamburg, Kumla, Sandholt & Sønderby, Madrid, Milan and Los Angeles show improving EBITDA margins, while Duiven, Alkmaar, Barcelona and Wellma Europe show dilution. Customer concentration often rises sharply into 2026, making delivery of the forecast more dependent on a small number of accounts.

All sequences below follow 2023A → 2026LF → 2031E. Financial amounts are in €m, unless stated otherwise. “Post-capex conversion” means (EBITDA less all capex) / EBITDA; it excludes working capital, tax and financing costs.

Two qualifications apply throughout:

* The customer analysis uses the displayed “Top 10” cohort. The images do not establish whether these are re-ranked each year; a low 2023 share may therefore reflect the historical size of forecast key accounts rather than genuine diversification.
* Format/customer relationships with margins are inferences, as the images do not provide profitability by format or customer, production volumes, utilisation or unit costs.

Duiven (1 & 2)

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €10.1m → €42.0m → €80.3m, with EBITDA rising €2.1m → €6.1m → €11.2m. However, gross margin falls 45.1% → 36.7% → 36.2%, and EBITDA margin falls 20.6% → 14.6% → 14.0%: substantial growth comes with materially lower profitability per euro of revenue.
* Capex: Combined maintenance and expansion capex intensity declines approximately 7.7% → 4.0% → 3.2%, supporting improving post-capex conversion of 62.7% → 72.8% → 77.1%, despite EBITDA margin dilution.
* Format concentration: Powder remains dominant but declines 71.7% → 68.3% → 67.0%, while gummies & bars increase 14.3% → 28.5% → 30.3%. These two formats account for approximately 97% of revenue by 2031.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share rises 61% → 87%, before easing to 68%. Customer 1 grows from zero recorded revenue to 57.6% in 2026 and 44.4% in 2031; its revenue nevertheless continues increasing €24.2m → €35.7m.
* Overlay: The major margin decline coincides with the emergence of Customer 1 and a larger gummies & bars mix. This is consistent with lower-margin incremental business, pricing concessions or ramp-up costs. However, margins remain subdued as customer concentration eases after 2026, suggesting that diversification alone does not reverse the dilution.

Enschede

* Operational performance: Revenue grows €7.3m → €25.0m → €58.5m, and EBITDA €1.4m → €4.6m → €11.2m. Gross margin compresses substantially 65.1% → 51.2% → 48.1%, while EBITDA margin remains comparatively resilient at 19.6% → 18.6% → 19.2%.
* Capex: Post-capex conversion improves 65.4% → 93.5% → 80.7%. The unusually high 2026 conversion reflects zero expansion capex; expansion investment returns to €1.2m in 2031.
* Format concentration: Capsules & tablets increase 80.1% → 89.4% → 89.8%, while “Other” declines 13.3% → 7.3% → 6.0%. The site becomes more specialised.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share increases 34% → 69% → 54%. Customer 1 rises 1.8% → 42.1% → 33.2%, becoming the principal disclosed growth account.
* Overlay: Greater format specialisation and customer dependence coincide with gross-margin dilution. However, the gap between gross and EBITDA margins narrows from 45.5pp to 28.9pp, consistent with operating-cost leverage offsetting much of that dilution. The forecast depends on scale efficiencies compensating for lower gross profitability.

Alkmaar

* Operational performance: Revenue rises €5.5m → €14.8m → €35.6m, and EBITDA €2.1m → €4.1m → €9.8m. Gross margin is broadly stable 65.3% → 67.1% → 66.3%, but EBITDA margin drops 38.3% → 27.7% → 27.4%.
* Capex: 2026 is investment-heavy: maintenance capex reaches 4.6% of revenue and expansion capex 10.1%, reducing post-capex conversion 95.2% → 47.1% → 83.2%. Investment intensity normalises by 2031.
* Format concentration: Gels decline 85.3% → 79.9% → 78.4%, while “Other” increases 11.3% → 20.1% → 21.6%. Diversification remains limited because gels still dominate.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share is 73% → 79% → 73%. The three largest displayed customers together account for approximately 48.4% → 58.6% → 56.6%, indicating greater concentration within the leading accounts despite the Top 10 share returning to its starting level.
* Overlay: Stable gross margins make adverse product pricing/mix a less convincing explanation for EBITDA margin deterioration. The gross-to-EBITDA margin gap widens 27.0pp → 39.4pp → 38.9pp, pointing instead towards higher operating costs below gross profit. The investment programme may accompany a capacity ramp-up, but capex itself does not explain EBITDA dilution, and the forecast shows little subsequent margin recovery.

Hamburg

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €6.8m → €17.0m → €43.0m, while EBITDA rises sharply €0.5m → €6.7m → €16.4m. EBITDA margin jumps 7.3% → 39.2% → 38.0%; gross margin moves 72.9% → 79.9% → 70.0%.
* Capex: Investment requirements remain modest, with maintenance capex around 0.5% of revenue in both forecast periods. Post-capex conversion improves 41.0% → 91.9% → 94.8%.
* Format concentration: Niche technologies increase 77.3% → 99.0% → 99.0%. However, 2023 includes a sizeable 16.7% reconciliation balance, so part of the apparent concentration increase reflects removal of an unallocated category.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share rises 55% → 83% → 76%. The two largest displayed customers account for 30.3% → 55.3% → 53.4%, with Customer 1 increasing 3.0% → 32.6% → 31.0%.
* Overlay: The forecast assumes a substantial improvement in operating efficiency alongside niche-technology specialisation and larger key accounts. From 2026 to 2031, a 9.9pp gross-margin decline translates into only 1.2pp EBITDA-margin dilution, implying further significant cost leverage. This is a particularly important assumption to validate; the margin uplift is not explained by format mix alone.

Kumla

* Operational performance: Revenue grows €11.5m → €16.1m → €28.5m, and EBITDA €4.2m → €6.8m → €12.1m. Gross margin increases then moderates 67.2% → 72.1% → 69.3%, while EBITDA margin improves 36.9% → 42.2% → 42.4%.
* Capex: Maintenance capex remains below 1% of revenue throughout. Post-capex conversion stays strong at 98.3% → 93.0% → 95.6%, despite expansion investment in the forecast periods.
* Format concentration: Capsules & tablets increase 79.0% → 89.7% → 91.3%. The 2023 11.0% reconciliation balance limits the comparability of the initial mix.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share increases 73% → 84% → 76%. Customers 1 and 2 together increase 47.1% → 60.2% → 57.1%; Customer 2’s share rises steadily 11.5% → 19.7% → 20.5%.
* Overlay: Greater specialisation and larger anchor accounts coincide with higher EBITDA margins, consistent with production efficiencies and better absorption of operating costs. However, over half of forecast revenue depends on two disclosed customers, making retention and commercial terms important to sustaining those margins.

Sandholt & Sønderby

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €17.8m → €28.4m → €49.7m, and EBITDA €2.0m → €4.1m → €9.0m. Gross margin declines 73.5% → 64.8% → 63.7%, but EBITDA margin improves 11.2% → 14.3% → 18.2%.
* Capex: Expansion capex intensity falls 11.6% → 1.9% → 1.9%. Post-capex conversion improves from −21.9% → 81.6% → 84.5%: 2023 EBITDA less all capex is approximately −€0.4m, indicating a material investment burden in the base year.
* Format concentration: “Other” declines 58.0% → 48.4% → 29.5%, while gels increase 19.9% → 30.5% → 44.2% and powder 11.5% → 20.9% → 26.1%. Gels and powder together reach 70.3% by 2031.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share rises 63% → 70% → 74%. Customers 1 and 2 together increase 41.3% → 49.5% → 57.3%; Customer 1 reaches 42.6% by 2031.
* Overlay: The growing gels/powder mix accompanies lower gross margins, but EBITDA improves through assumed operating-cost leverage. The gross-to-EBITDA margin gap narrows 62.3pp → 50.5pp → 45.5pp. The investment profile is consistent with benefits from earlier expenditure, although utilisation data would be needed to substantiate that explanation. Customer dependence increases as margins improve.

Barcelona

* Operational performance: Revenue contracts €22.1m → €16.5m, then recovers to €27.0m. EBITDA moves €4.5m → €2.8m → €4.4m, remaining below 2023 despite higher 2031 revenue. Gross margin is relatively stable 46.5% → 48.8% → 48.3%, but EBITDA margin declines 20.5% → 16.8% → 16.2%.
* Capex: Post-capex conversion is 81.3% → 72.9% → 83.1%. Expansion capex declines in absolute terms across the periods, helping conversion recover by 2031.
* Format concentration: Liquids increase 61.8% → 82.8% → 79.2%, while capsules & tablets rise 3.9% → 10.7% → 15.0%. “Other” declines sharply 21.0% → 5.2% → 3.9%.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share increases 58% → 90% → 89%. Customers 1 and 2 together rise 51.4% → 76.4% → 78.2%. Customer 1’s share falls after 2026, but Customer 2’s rises 23.0% → 36.6%, leaving overall dependence on the pair even higher.
* Overlay: The 2026 contraction is consistent with weaker cost absorption: gross margin improves while EBITDA margin falls. However, the absence of an EBITDA-margin recovery as revenue rebounds suggests a persistent cost burden or less profitable incremental accounts. Customer 2 contributes approximately €6.1m of the €10.5m forecast revenue increase after 2026, making its economics especially relevant.

Madrid

* Operational performance: Revenue grows €4.1m → €9.5m → €27.3m, and EBITDA €1.0m → €3.3m → €10.0m. Gross margin improves 62.0% → 66.6% → 65.9%, and EBITDA margin expands 25.2% → 34.9% → 36.6%.
* Capex: Expansion capex intensity declines 4.3% → 3.8% → 3.5%, with post-capex conversion improving 80.1% → 85.0% → 86.9%.
* Format concentration: Gummies & bars account for effectively all revenue throughout. The displayed 2023 share of 101.3% is offset by a negative reconciliation balance; it should be interpreted as approximately complete specialisation.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share increases 16% → 80% → 60%. Customer 1 rises from negligible recorded revenue to 52.2% in 2026, then declines to 36.5% in 2031 as other revenue grows faster.
* Overlay: Because format mix is effectively unchanged, forecast margin improvement is more plausibly associated with scale, customer economics or production efficiency. The anchor account appears central to the initial growth step; subsequent diversification accompanies further EBITDA-margin improvement, although single-format exposure remains complete.

Milan

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €4.7m → €6.2m → €11.4m, and EBITDA €0.6m → €1.1m → €2.3m. Gross margin improves 75.0% → 78.0% → 78.0%, while EBITDA margin expands 12.3% → 18.4% → 20.0%.
* Capex: Expansion capex falls sharply from 25.2% of revenue → 3.5% → 2.5%. Post-capex conversion improves −125.8% → 57.5% → 74.5%, although maintenance capex remains relatively material.
* Format concentration: Niche technologies remain approximately 98% of revenue throughout. Negative “Other” and positive reconciliation entries complicate the 2023 split, but the specialisation is clear.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share declines 76% → 73% → 52%. Customer 2 falls 25.1% → 12.5% → 5.3%, including an absolute revenue decline €1.2m → €0.8m → €0.6m; Customer 1 peaks at 17.9% in 2026 before falling to 12.5%.
* Overlay: Improving EBITDA margins alongside stable format mix and declining customer concentration support a scale/efficiency interpretation rather than increased dependence on one anchor account. Growth increasingly comes from outside the displayed cohort. The strong 2023 investment burden is consistent with a development phase, but its subsequent contribution cannot be confirmed from the images.

Bridgwater

* Operational performance: Revenue grows €10.2m → €13.2m → €24.9m, and EBITDA €3.0m → €3.0m → €5.9m. Gross margin improves 52.3% → 55.1% → 57.6%, but EBITDA margin declines 29.6% → 23.0% → 23.7%.
* Capex: Expansion capex intensity rises 3.0% → 7.4% → 2.5%, driving post-capex conversion 88.8% → 64.7% → 84.4%.
* Format concentration: Gels increase 37.6% → 40.2% → 43.4%, and powder 21.6% → 24.3% → 31.9%. Together they reach 75.3% by 2031, while “Other” declines to 24.3%.
* Customer concentration: The relevant customer table is not visible in the supplied images.
* Overlay: The gels/powder shift coincides with improving gross profitability, but this benefit is more than offset by costs below gross profit. The gross-to-EBITDA margin gap widens 22.7pp → 32.1pp → 33.9pp. The 2026 investment peak may accompany a ramp-up, but forecast EBITDA margins remain below 2023 even after investment intensity falls.

Los Angeles

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €13.2m → €23.6m → €48.3m, and EBITDA €1.2m → €4.8m → €10.4m. Gross margin rises 48.0% → 61.0% → 59.3%, while EBITDA margin expands 8.7% → 20.3% → 21.5%.
* Capex: Maintenance capex remains high at 5.0% → 3.2% → 4.7% of revenue. Expansion capex is 0.0% → 5.2% → 3.0%, with post-capex conversion improving 43.0% → 58.8% → 64.3% but remaining comparatively low.
* Format concentration: Forecast revenue is 100% niche technologies. However, the format table shows zero 2023 revenue, despite material revenue in the operational table; historical format evolution therefore cannot be assessed reliably.
* Customer concentration: The relevant customer table is not visible in the supplied images.
* Overlay: The forecast combines a substantial gross-margin reset with further operating leverage. Niche-technology specialisation may support the projected profitability, but the missing historical format data prevents a mix-based explanation. High maintenance requirements also mean EBITDA growth translates less fully into post-capex earnings than at Hamburg or Kumla.

Wellma Europe

* Operational performance: Revenue grows €23.6m → €68.0m → €157.9m, and EBITDA €4.5m → €10.3m → €22.8m. Gross margin declines 43.1% → 40.8% → 38.6%, and EBITDA margin 19.1% → 15.2% → 14.4%.
* Capex: Investment remains modest relative to EBITDA, with post-capex conversion 95.9% → 94.0% → 93.9%. The displayed negative 2026 capex intensities appear to be sign errors: the positive capex amounts imply approximately +0.2% maintenance and +0.7% expansion intensity.
* Format concentration: Capsules & tablets remain dominant 76.2% → 78.2% → 77.3%. Powder moves 15.7% → 8.3% → 12.2%, while gummies & bars rise from negligible revenue to 5.8%, then moderate to 3.3%.
* Customer concentration: The relevant customer table is not visible in the supplied images.
* Overlay: Broadly stable capsule/tablet concentration makes a major format shift an insufficient explanation for sustained margin dilution. Lower gross profitability appears to account for most of the EBITDA-margin decline, suggesting pricing, input costs or changes within formats/customer accounts merit investigation. Strong post-capex conversion partly offsets weaker margins.

Points to resolve before using this as a firm investment conclusion

* Currency consistency: Kumla and Sandholt format amounts appear to remain in local currencies, while Bridgwater and Los Angeles also have different revenue bases between sections. I have used operational-table euro amounts and the displayed mix percentages, without treating cross-section absolute amounts as directly comparable.
* Reconciliation effects: Several 2023 format tables contain sizeable reconciliation balances, so some apparent specialisation reflects allocation changes.
* Missing customer data: Customer-concentration overlays for Bridgwater, Los Angeles and Wellma Europe require the remaining customer-table images.
* Most consequential forecast assumptions: Hamburg requires substantial operating-cost leverage; Alkmaar and Bridgwater show rising costs below gross profit despite resilient/improving gross margins; Barcelona becomes highly dependent on two accounts without recovering its historical EBITDA margin.

Source: operational metrics in IMG_8177–8179; format splits in IMG_8180(1)–8183; customer splits in IMG_8184–8186. Interpretations and combined percentages are derived from those figures.

Bridgwater

* Operational performance: Revenue increases €10.2m → €13.2m → €24.9m, while EBITDA grows €3.0m → €3.0m → €5.9m. Gross margin improves 52.3% → 55.1% → 57.6%, but EBITDA margin falls 29.6% → 23.0% → 23.7%, indicating that higher costs below gross profit outweigh the improvement in gross profitability.
* Format concentration: Gels and powder together increase 59.2% → 64.5% → 75.3% of revenue. “Other” becomes less significant by 2031, leaving growth increasingly concentrated in the two core formats.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share moves 54% → 58% → 51%. Customer 1 declines 21.7% → 11.6% → 11.9%, including an absolute revenue reduction €2.2m → €1.5m before recovering to €3.0m. Customers 2 and 4 become more important, together increasing 7.9% → 18.1% → 16.6%.
* Growth composition: From 2026 to 2031, revenue from the displayed Top 10 increases approximately €5.2m, versus total site growth of €11.6m. Approximately 55% of incremental revenue therefore comes from outside the displayed cohort, supporting broader customer diversification.
* Overlay onto margins: The initial loss of Customer 1 revenue coincides with EBITDA-margin compression and could contribute to poorer cost absorption or a change in customer economics. However, rising gross margins suggest the main pressure sits below gross profit. Subsequent customer diversification and greater gels/powder exposure support growth, but the limited EBITDA-margin recovery implies a persistent operating-cost burden. The 2026 expansion-capex peak also temporarily reduces post-capex conversion to 64.7%, recovering to 84.4% in 2031.

Los Angeles

* Operational performance: Between 2026LF and 2031E, revenue increases €23.6m → €48.3m, and EBITDA €4.8m → €10.4m. Gross margin declines 61.0% → 59.3%, while EBITDA margin improves 20.3% → 21.5%, implying operating-cost leverage offsets gross-margin dilution.
* Format concentration: Forecast revenue is 100% niche technologies in both periods, leaving the site fully exposed to one format category.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 accounts for 96% → 95% of revenue. Customer 1 remains dominant at 62.8% → 61.6%, while the two largest customers together account for approximately 73% in both periods. The first four customers increase their combined share 86.9% → 88.0%.
* Growth composition: Customer 1 revenue approximately doubles €14.8m → €29.8m, contributing around 60% of total forecast revenue growth. Its slightly declining percentage share therefore provides little reduction in absolute exposure.
* Overlay onto margins: Forecast EBITDA-margin improvement depends heavily on scaling production for a small number of customers within one format. Larger repeat programmes could support utilisation and cost absorption, but customer loss, repricing or volume shortfalls would directly threaten that operating leverage. High capex requirements further limit post-capex conversion to 58.8% → 64.3%, despite improving EBITDA margins.
* Historical qualification: The newly supplied customer table explicitly states that the site was not operational in 2023, consistent with zero revenue in the format table. The earlier 2023 operational baseline should not be used to infer historical growth or margin improvement until reconciled.

Wellma Europe

* Operational performance: Revenue rises €23.6m → €68.0m → €157.9m, and EBITDA €4.5m → €10.3m → €22.8m. Gross margin declines 43.1% → 40.8% → 38.6%, alongside EBITDA-margin dilution 19.1% → 15.2% → 14.4%.
* Format concentration: Capsules & tablets remain broadly stable at 76.2% → 78.2% → 77.3%. The absence of a major format shift suggests changes within formats, customer economics or input costs are more relevant to margin dilution.
* Customer concentration: The displayed Top 10 share increases 47% → 73%, before easing to 61%. Customer 5, initially the largest displayed account, declines 26.5% → 8.5% → 7.9%; Customer 1 becomes the largest at 5.5% → 20.0% → 17.7%.
* Customer-base transition: Customer 5 revenue falls €6.2m → €5.8m, despite substantial overall growth into 2026, before recovering to €12.5m in 2031. Meanwhile, Customers 1–4 collectively increase €2.5m → €33.0m → €63.1m, demonstrating that the forecast relies heavily on a different set of growth accounts.
* Overlay onto margins: The transition towards larger contributions from Customers 1–4 coincides with gross- and EBITDA-margin dilution. This is consistent with lower-margin incremental business or commercial concessions, although customer profitability is needed to establish causation. Concentration eases after 2026 while margins continue falling, so customer diversification alone does not improve the forecast economics.
* Cash implications: Low capex requirements preserve approximately 94% post-capex conversion in the forecast periods. That supports cash generation before working capital, tax and financing, but does not offset the underlying reduction in profitability per euro of revenue.

